window.docContents[92].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___2":{"contents":"As a final example consider the representation theory of finite groups, which is one of the most\nfascinating chapters of representation theory. In this theory, one considers representations of\nthe group algebra A = \\mathbb{C}[G] of a finite group G — the algebra with basis a_g,\ng \\in G, and multiplication law a_g a_h = a_{gh}. We will show that any finite dimensional\nrepresentation of A is a direct sum of irreducible representations, i.e., the notions of an\nirreducible and indecomposable representation are the same for A (Maschke's theorem). Another\nstriking result discussed below is the Frobenius divisibility theorem: the dimension of any\nirreducible representation of A divides the order of G. Finally, we will show how to use the\nrepresentation theory of finite groups to prove Burnside's theorem: any finite group of order\np^a q^b, where p, q are primes, is solvable. Note that this theorem does not mention\nrepresentations, which are used only in its proof; a purely group-theoretical proof of this\ntheorem (not using representations) exists but is much more difficult!\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Dynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview","header":"Dynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___2"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/#chapter-03":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory","header":"3. General results of representation theory","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/#chapter-03"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An Artinian and Noetherian module has a finite internal spanning family satisfying the displayed module property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=primary]\n\nTwo finite internal spanning families satisfying the displayed property have equal lengths and equivalent members after a permutation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length\u0009Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra--Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Computes a coefficient of a product as a finite sum of coefficients evaluated at an element and at its inverse translate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Remark4.5.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Characters defined without representations via convolution algebra\u0009Characters defined without representations via convolution algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra--Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups--Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: Nat.Partition\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting\n\nA second auxiliary type indexed by a natural number and a partition of it.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary subgroup of the permutations of a finite type, indexed by a partition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting]\n\nA second auxiliary subgroup of the permutations of a finite type, indexed by a partition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting]\n\nSelects an object of the auxiliary source type for each partition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting]\n\nSelects an object of the auxiliary target type for each partition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.12.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009Partition, Young diagram, Young tableau, row and column subgroups\u0009Partition, Young diagram, Young tableau, row and column subgroups\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups--Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Section-5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___13___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009Section 5.13: Proof of the classification theorem for representations of S_n\u0009Section 5.13: Proof of the classification theorem for representations of S_n","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Section-5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___13___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14___heading-1":{"contents":"Denote by U_\\lambda the representation \\operatorname{Ind}_{P_\\lambda}^{S_n} \\mathbb{C}. It is easy to see that U_\\lambda can be alternatively defined as U_\\lambda = \\mathbb{C}[S_n] a_\\lambda.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Section 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda\u0009Section 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda","header":"5.14. Induced representations for  S_n","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Remark5___8___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Dimension of induced representation equals dim V times index (G:H)\u0009Dimension of induced representation equals dim V times index (G:H)","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Remark5___8___3___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Definition-6___5___1___-Dimension-vector/#Chapter6___Definition6___5___1":{"contents":"Definition 6.5.1. Let Q be a quiver with any labeling 1, \\ldots, n of the vertices. Let V = (V_1, \\ldots, V_n) be a representation of Q. We then call\n\nd(V) = (\\dim V_1, \\ldots, \\dim V_n)\n\nthe dimension vector of this representation.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.5. Gabriel's theorem\u0009Definition 6.5.1: Dimension vector","header":"Definition 6.5.1: Dimension vector","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Definition-6___5___1___-Dimension-vector/#Chapter6___Definition6___5___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2___heading-1":{"contents":"We would like to define arrows between categories. Such arrows are called functors.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.2. Functors\u0009Section 7.2: Functors\u0009Section 7.2: Functors","header":"7.2. Functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2___heading-1"}});